// A write through an exported `word[]`. `idstd`'s lset takes an `int[]` and
// this list holds addresses, so there has to be one at this width too -- the
// same function at a different type, which the uniqueness rule allows because
// the signature differs. The list is named `cells` rather than `xs` for the
// duller reason: `xs` is an `int[]` elsewhere in this program and a name may
// not be two types.
wset(word[] cells, int i, word a) {
  cells[i] = a;
} return void;

// One pixel, at 32 bits per pixel: blue, green, red, unused. Out-of-range
// coordinates are dropped rather than trapped, because a rasteriser clips by
// drawing and asking a caller to clip first is how a kernel gets a page fault
// from a rounding error.
fb_px(word x, word y, word c) {
  if(x < fb_w() && y < fb_h()) {
    poke32(fb_base() + (y * fb_w() + x) * 4, c);
  }
} return void;

// The same write at the other width. `idstd` has `lset`, and a freestanding
// build has no `idstd` -- so the kernel carries its own, beside the one for
// addresses. Two functions with one body, which the uniqueness rule allows
// only because the signatures differ; there is no way to write it once.
lset(int[] xs, int i, int v) {
  xs[i] = v;
} return void;
